2. JavaScript in <head>

JavaScript can be written inside the <head> section of an HTML document using the <script> tag.

JavaScript code ko HTML ke <head> section ke andar bhi likh sakte hain.

Syntax
<head>

    <script>
        // JavaScript code
    </script>

</head>

<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <title>JavaScript in Head</title>
    <script>
        function showMessage() {
            alert("JavaScript is written inside Head!");
        }
    </script>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>JavaScript in Head Example</h1>
    <button onclick="showMessage()">
        Click Me
    </button>
</body>
</html>

<script src="script.js" defer></script>
3. JavaScript in <body>
JavaScript can be written inside the <body> section of an HTML document using the <script> tag.

JavaScript ko HTML ke <body> ke andar bhi likh sakte hain. Agar script ko body ke end mein rakha jaye, to generally HTML elements pehle load ho chuke hote hain.

<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <title>JavaScript in Body</title>
</head>
<body>

    <h1 id="heading">
        JavaScript in Body
    </h1>

    <button onclick="changeText()">
        Change Text
    </button>
    <script>
        function changeText() {
            document.getElementById("heading").innerHTML =
                "Text Changed Using JavaScript!";
        }
    </script>
</body>
</html>

5. External JavaScript

External JavaScript is a method of writing JavaScript code in a separate .js file and linking it to an HTML document using the <script> tag.

External JavaScript mein JavaScript code ko HTML file ke andar nahi likhte. JavaScript ke liye separate .js file banate hain aur HTML mein us file ko connect karte hain.

Example Structure
project/
│
├── index.html
│
└── script.js

<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">

<head>

    <meta charset="UTF-8">

    <title>External JavaScript</title>

    <script src="script.js" defer></script>

</head>

<body>

    <h1 id="heading">
        External JavaScript Demo
    </h1>

    <button onclick="changeHeading()">
        Change Heading
    </button>

</body>

</html>

JavaScript File — script.js
function changeHeading() {

    document.getElementById("heading").innerHTML =
        "Text Changed From External JavaScript!";

}

6. External JavaScript Reference
The src attribute of the <script> tag is used to specify the path or URL of an external JavaScript file.

src attribute ka use HTML document ko external JavaScript file ka address/path batane ke liye hota hai.

Syntax
<script src="script.js"></script>

6.1 Same Folder External JavaScript

Agar HTML aur JS file same folder mein hain:

project/
├── index.html
└── script.js

Use:

<script src="script.js"></script>

6.2 Folder ke andar JavaScript

Structure:

project/
├── index.html
└── js/
    └── script.js

Reference:

<script src="js1/js/script.js"></script>

6.3 Parent Folder ki JavaScript

Structure:

project/
├── script.js
└── pages/
    └── index.html

index.html se:

<script src="../script.js"></script>

../ means go to the parent directory.

7. External JavaScript Advantages

1. Code Reusability--:

The same JavaScript file can be used by multiple HTML pages.

index.html ──┐
about.html ──┼──> script.js
contact.html ┘
2. Separation of Code--:

External JavaScript separates HTML structure from JavaScript logic.
HTML → Structure
CSS  → Design
JS   → Logic / Behaviour

3. Easy Maintenance--:
External JavaScript makes the code easier to maintain and update.

4. Better Code Organization--:
External JavaScript helps organize large applications into separate files and modules.
Large project mein code ko properly organize karna easy hota hai.

// add function

5. Reusable Logic--:
Common functions and logic can be written once and reused on multiple pages.


6. Browser Caching--
Browsers can cache external JavaScript files, which may reduce repeated downloads.

Browser external JS file ko cache kar sakta hai, jisse repeated page visits par file ko dobara download karne ki need kam ho sakti hai.

JavaScript Function--:

A function is a reusable block of JavaScript code designed to perform a specific task.

Function JavaScript code ka ek reusable block hai jo kisi specific task ko perform karta hai.

Syntax
function functionName() {

    // code

}
Example
function greet() {

    console.log("Hello Student");

}

Function ko execute/call karne ke liye:

greet();

9. Function with Parameters--:

Parameters are variables defined in a function that receive values when the function is called.


Example
function greet(name) {

    console.log("Hello " + name);

}

greet("AtoZ");

Output:

Hello Adil

Yahan:

name → Parameter
"Adil" → Argument

10. Function with Return Value--:
A return statement sends a value from a function back to the code that called the function.
return function se result/value ko wapas caller ke paas bhejta hai.

Example
function add(a, b) {

    return a + b;

}

let result = add(10, 20);

console.log(result);

Output:

30

11. JavaScript Event--:

An event is an action or occurrence detected by the browser, such as a mouse click, key press, form submission, or page loading.

Event browser mein hone wala koi action/occurrence hai jise JavaScript detect karke response de sakti hai.

Common                  Events
Event	                Meaning
click	                Element par click
dblclick	            Double click
mouseover	            Mouse element ke upar aana
mouseout	            Mouse element se bahar jana
keydown	                Keyboard key press
keyup	                Keyboard key release
submit	                Form submit
change	                Input value change
input	                Input mein value enter/change
load	                Page/resource load
focus	                Element active/focused hona
blur	                Element ka focus lose hona

12. Event Handling--:
Event handling is the process of detecting an event and executing JavaScript code in response to that event.

Example
<button onclick="showMessage()">
    Click Me
</button>
function showMessage() {

    alert("Button Clicked!");

}

Flow:

User Clicks Button
       ↓
click Event
       ↓
showMessage()
       ↓
alert()
       ↓
Message Display

13. Event Listener--:
addEventListener() is a JavaScript method used to attach an event handler to an HTML element.
addEventListener() ka use HTML element ke saath event ko connect karne ke liye hota hai.

Example
const button = document.getElementById("btn");

button.addEventListener("click", function() {

    alert("Button Clicked!");

});

14. Complete Function + Event + DOM Example

<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">

<head>

    <meta charset="UTF-8">

    <meta name="viewport"
          content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">

    <title>JavaScript Function and Event</title>

    <style>

        body {
            font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
            text-align: center;
            padding: 50px;
            background: #f4f7fb;
        }

        .box {
            max-width: 500px;
            margin: auto;
            padding: 30px;
            background: white;
            border-radius: 15px;
        }

        button {
            padding: 12px 25px;
            border: none;
            border-radius: 8px;
            cursor: pointer;
            font-size: 16px;
        }

        #output {
            margin-top: 20px;
            font-size: 20px;
        }

    </style>

</head>

<body>

    <div class="box">

        <h1 id="heading">
            JavaScript Function
        </h1>

        <button id="btn">
            Click Me
        </button>

        <p id="output">
            Waiting for event...
        </p>

    </div>


    <script>

        // Function

        function showMessage() {

            document.getElementById("heading").innerHTML =
                "Welcome to JavaScript!";

            document.getElementById("output").innerHTML =
                "The button click event was handled successfully.";

        }


        // Event Listener

        const button = document.getElementById("btn");

        button.addEventListener("click", showMessage);

    </script>

</body>

</html>
